.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_GEFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_gefs
.Nd mount a gefs file system, or one of its snapshots
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar special node
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
attaches a label of the gefs file system on the block device
.Ar special
to the directory
.Ar node .
A gefs file system holds several trees, each named by a label:
.Dq main
by default, forks (mutable copies) and snapshots (read-only).
The labels of one device may be mounted at the same time on different
nodes; they share one block cache and one set of kernel threads, and each
mount has its own device number, so files that a fork shares with its
base are not taken for hard links of each other.
A label is mounted at most once.
.Pp
The options are those of
.Xr mount 8
.Pq Cm ro , rw , update , noatime , nodev , nosuid , noexec , sync , async , force
and:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm snap= Ns Ar label
Mount
.Ar label
instead of
.Dq main .
A snapshot may only be mounted read-only.
The label
.Dq dump
(9front's view of all the snapshots) is not supported; mount the
snapshots themselves.
.It Cm cache= Ns Ar MiB
The size of the block cache, when this mount is the first of its device
(default 64).
.El
.Pp
Access times are updated at most once a day unless the file changed
since
.Pq Dq relatime ,
and reach the disk with the next commit that happens anyway.
.Sh POWER
gefs writes nothing while nothing changes.
A change is committed once it is
.Va vfs.gefs.interval
seconds old, earlier when space runs low, and at once for
.Xr fsync 2 ,
.Xr sync 2
and unmount; file data goes into the tree when the syncer writes its
pages back, and one commit (two device cache flushes) takes all of it.
.Xr fsync 2
calls that wait at the same time share one commit, and none returns
before its data is on the disk.
An idle mount does no disk I/O at all; automatic snapshots are taken by
such commits and only for labels that changed.
.Pp
These
.Xr sysctl 8
variables apply to every gefs mount:
.Bl -tag -width vfs.gefs.groupwait
.It Va vfs.gefs.interval
Seconds a change may wait for a commit, at most (default 30).
A longer interval puts more changes into one disk wake-up, and a crash
loses more of them.
Data written with
.Xr write 2
reaches the tree when the syncer writes its pages back
.Pq Va kern.syncdelay ,
so without
.Xr fsync 2
it can wait about the interval plus that.
.It Va vfs.gefs.groupwait
Microseconds a commit asked for by
.Xr fsync 2
waits for more callers to join (default 0).
.It Va vfs.gefs.dirtypct
Commit when the blocks queued for writing pass this percentage of the
block cache (default 0, never).
.It Va vfs.gefs.lazymax
Seconds that access times and the frees a commit leaves for the next one
may wait alone for a commit (default 300).
They ride with any other commit; this bounds what a crash leaks
when nothing else changes, so on battery it can follow
.Va vfs.gefs.interval
upwards.
.It Va vfs.gefs.commits , skipped , requests , joined
Commits written, skipped as clean, durability requests, and requests
that shared another's commit.
.It Va vfs.gefs.commits_sync , commits_age , commits_dirty , commits_space , commits_lazy
Commits by reason.
.It Va vfs.gefs.reads , writes , flushes
Device block reads and writes, and cache flushes.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Mount the file system, a fork of it for build output, and yesterday's
snapshot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mount -t gefs /dev/dk1 /
mount -t gefs -o snap=obj /dev/dk1 /usr/obj
mount -t gefs -o ro,snap=main@day.2026.09.27_00:00:00 /dev/dk1 /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Xr fstab 5
entries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NAME=gefsroot	/		gefs	rw		0 0
NAME=gefsroot	/usr/obj	gefs	rw,snap=obj	0 0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr gefs 8 ,
.Xr mount 8
.Sh HISTORY
gefs was written by Ori Bernstein for 9front.
The
.Nx
port appeared in 2026.
